Q: How can I get EnumWindows to list all windows? A: <p>Building on the original answer’s <code>EnumWindows</code> approach, here is an alternative that uses <code>std::string</code> instead of manual <code>new[]</code> and <code>delete[]</code>.</p>
<p>This gives automatic memory management, removes the need for explicit cleanup, and reduces the risk of memory leaks.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;Windows.h&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

using namespace std;

static BOOL CALLBACK enumWindowCallback(HWND hWnd, LPARAM)
{
    int titleLength = GetWindowTextLengthA(hWnd);

    if (IsWindowVisible(hWnd) &amp;&amp; titleLength != 0)
    {
        string currentWindowTitle(titleLength + 1, '\0');

        GetWindowTextA(
            hWnd,
            &amp;currentWindowTitle[0],
            titleLength + 1
        );

        cout &lt;&lt; hWnd &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; currentWindowTitle.c_str() &lt;&lt; endl;
    }

    return TRUE;
}

int main()
{
    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Enumerating windows...&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;

    EnumWindows(enumWindowCallback, NULL);

    cin.ignore();
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to test Vue ripple directive has been used? A: <p>Your mock is not being called because <code>v-ripple</code> has already been registered by the Quasar plugin in your global test setup:</p>
<p><code>config.global.plugins = [[Quasar, { plugins: { LocalStorage } }]]</code></p>
<p>By the time you call <code>mount()</code>, Vue is already using <em>Quasar's</em> directive, so the one you pass in <code>global.directives</code> is never used.</p>
<p>If you just want to make sure that your component applies the directive do not install Quasar for that test. Instead, register a stub directive and spy on its lifecycle hook:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { mount } from '@vue/test-utils'
import { vi, expect } from 'vitest'

const beforeMount = vi.fn()

mount(MyComponent, {
  global: {
    directives: {
      ripple: {
        beforeMount,
      },
    },
  },
})

expect(beforeMount).toHaveBeenCalled()
</code></pre>
<p>Also you registered the directive as <code>Ripple</code>, and it should be <code>ripple</code>.</p>
<p>If Quasar is installed globally, this approach won't work because Quasar's own <code>v-ripple</code> registration takes precedence. In that case you'd need to either avoid installing Quasar for this specific test or take a different testing approach (e.g: assert the directive exists).</p>

Q: Race conditions &quot;check-then-act&quot; and &quot;read-modify-write&quot; A: <p>What is a race condition?
Check this stack-overflow <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/34510/what-is-a-race-condition">question</a>.</p>
<p>There are primarily two scenarios for race-condition: read-modify-write and check-then-act.</p>
<p>For read-modify-write classical example is of <code>counter++</code> which is not an atomic operation so leads to race condition.</p>
<p>For check-then-act there are multiple examples. One example is when you check for key existence in HashMap and then do some work in if-case. Another example is singleton class code:</p>
<pre><code>public Singleton getInstance() {
   if (_instance == null) { 
      _instance = new Singleton();
   }
   return _instance;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can read more about them on internet. One good book on concurrency is Java Concurrency in Practice by Brian Goetz.  You can also find this <a href="http://dig.cs.illinois.edu/papers/checkThenAct.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer">article</a> helpful.</p>

Q: How to create a multivariable polynomial using SymEngine C++? A: <p>Thanks — good catch and sorry for the confusion. You’re right to check the docs.</p>
<p>I made an incorrect assumption about a C++ Poly wrapper being available like in the Python API. The SymEngine C++ API does expose parsing, expressions (Basic), symbol handling (Symbol / free_symbols) and differentiation (diff), but it doesn’t provide the high-level Python-style Poly class in the same way (so my earlier example that constructed Poly in C++ was wrong).</p>
<p>Here’s a corrected, minimal answer you can post (copy/paste):</p>
<p>Use the C++ parser to get a RCP&lt;const Basic&gt; expression.</p>
<p>Use free_symbols(...) to collect variables.</p>
<p>Use diff(...) on the expression for derivatives.</p>
<p>If you need high-level polynomial-specific operations (polynomial arithmetic, factorization, multivariate Poly methods) from C++, either:</p>
<p>use the lower-level polynomial routines in SymEngine’s polys module (these are more manual and lower-level than Python’s Poly), or</p>
<p>use the SymEngine Python API (which does provide Poly) from Python, or</p>
<p>consider another C++ CAS (e.g., GiNaC) if you need a full Poly-style API in C++.</p>
<p>Example C++ snippet that omits the non-existent Poly and shows parsing + symbols + differentiation:<br />
thanks for reading!</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;set&gt;

#include &lt;symengine/basic.h&gt;
#include &lt;symengine/parser.h&gt;
#include &lt;symengine/printers/strprinter.h&gt;

using SymEngine::RCP;
using SymEngine::Basic;
using SymEngine::parse;
using SymEngine::StrPrinter;

int main() {
    std::string input;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Enter polynomial (e.g. x^2 + y^2 + x1*x2):\n&quot;;
    std::getline(std::cin, input);

    RCP&lt;const Basic&gt; expr;
    try {
        expr = parse(input);
    } catch (std::exception &amp;e) {
        std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Parse error: &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
        return 1;
    }

    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Parsed expression: &quot; &lt;&lt; StrPrinter::str(expr) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

    std::set&lt;RCP&lt;const Basic&gt;, SymEngine::RCPBasicKeyLess&gt; free_syms;
    expr-&gt;free_symbols(free_syms);

    if (free_syms.empty()) {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;No symbols found. Expression is constant.\n&quot;;
    } else {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Free symbols:\n&quot;;
        for (auto &amp;s : free_syms) {
            std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;  &quot; &lt;&lt; StrPrinter::str(s) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
        }
    }

    // Differentiate wrt each symbol (using Basic::diff)
    for (auto &amp;s : free_syms) {
        RCP&lt;const Basic&gt; deriv = expr-&gt;diff(s);
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;d/d&quot; &lt;&lt; StrPrinter::str(s) &lt;&lt; &quot;: &quot; &lt;&lt; StrPrinter::str(deriv) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
